   "changed": true,
   "text_before_clean": "How the Negroes of St. Lucia fought for freedom. By Arthur A. Schomburg.\nA new department: \"News of Women's Clubs,\" by Mrs. A. W. Hunton, formerly Organizer of the National Association of Colored Women's Clubs.\nOur circulation is growing rapidly, but we must have 25,000. Every reader of THE CRISIS can by personal solicitation help us to this goal. Probably no magazine in America has a more interesting set of readers—we have on our books judges, financiers, philanthropists, college presidents and scholars, besides the best educated colored people of the country.\n\nCOMMENTS ON THE CRISIS\n\n\"I have not yet expressed to you my opinion of the magazine which you are now editing. First of all, I admire the spirit of fairness in which it is conducted. The assumption that all parties are honest in their opinion is a long step forward in dealing with the race question. I am sure that while being conducted in such a spirit truth, be it pleasant or unpleasant, is what Negro journalism has wanted for many years. It is also a delightful thing to be able to place your paper into the hands of the younger generation and call their attention to the good English used in the pages. May THE CRISIS live long and accomplish its mission.\"",
   "role": "text",
   "split_from": "r9",
   "heading_restored": "COMMENTS ON THE CRISIS"
